WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.133
-You look sharp.
You look great.

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:04.100
-I look like
Herman Munster's friend.

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:06.033
[ Laughter ]
His best friend.

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:08.634
-His best friend you think
would -- Look at those guys.

00:00:08.634 --> 00:00:11.200
[ Crowd cheers ]
-Boom!

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.167
I've stomped a lot of
lanternflies with these shoes.

00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:16.167
[ Laughter ]
Yeah.

00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:18.100
-No, you would never hurt a --

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.167
That's what I always
said about you.

00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:20.434
You would never hurt
a lanternfly.

00:00:20.434 --> 00:00:23.801
-I am a gentle soul.
-You really are a gentle --

00:00:23.801 --> 00:00:25.968
Hey, this is your 13th
appearance on our show.

00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:29.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:29.534 --> 00:00:31.200
-Hope nothing falls on me.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.234
-No, it's not unlucky.

00:00:33.234 --> 00:00:35.968
Are you superstitious?
-A little bit.

00:00:35.968 --> 00:00:38.100
I don't like to walk
under ladders.

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:41.400
-Me neither.
-I always knock on wood.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:42.634
You don't --
-I do that too.

00:00:42.634 --> 00:00:44.000
-Yeah, yeah?
-Yeah.

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:45.834
I've walked under a ladder,
though, before.

00:00:45.834 --> 00:00:47.100
-And what happened?
-Nothing.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:48.868
-Nothing?
-Absolutely nothing.

00:00:48.868 --> 00:00:50.901
In fact --
-See, you got what you deserved.

00:00:50.901 --> 00:00:53.267
Nothing.
-Nothing.

00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:56.033
No, I was hosting
an MTV award show or something.

00:00:56.033 --> 00:00:58.834
I was so nervous to go on.
And then they go,

00:00:58.834 --> 00:01:00.601
"Oh, just go around this way
to get to the stage."

00:01:00.601 --> 00:01:02.501
I go, "Okay."
And I went around

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:04.200
and it was a giant ladder.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:07.334
So it looked like a, you know,
like just an archway.

00:01:07.334 --> 00:01:08.801
And I walked in and
this guy was working,

00:01:08.801 --> 00:01:10.367
and he goes,
"Oh, that's not good."

00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:12.000
[ Laughter ]

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.801
You can't say that to me.
And I walked under the ladder,

00:01:14.801 --> 00:01:17.400
but it turned out I had a great
show and nothing happened.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.801
So I don't know
if that superstition

00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:20.701
quite worked for me.
-Well, maybe it's silly.

00:01:20.701 --> 00:01:22.567
Maybe I shouldn't
worry about it.

00:01:22.567 --> 00:01:24.267
-I wouldn't worry about.
Oh, you know what else?

00:01:24.267 --> 00:01:26.767
Have you ever opened
an umbrella indoors?

00:01:26.767 --> 00:01:28.734
-Yeah, that one doesn't
bother me so much.

00:01:28.734 --> 00:01:29.934
-Yeah, I don't believe
that either.

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:31.701
You know, I found out
the story behind that.

00:01:31.701 --> 00:01:33.434
-What?
-Could you guess what the story

00:01:33.434 --> 00:01:35.634
is behind the umbrella
superstition?

00:01:35.634 --> 00:01:37.701
-Does it involve
somebody getting hurt?

00:01:37.701 --> 00:01:38.701
-No.
-No?

00:01:38.701 --> 00:01:40.501
-Does anyone
else know the story?

00:01:40.501 --> 00:01:43.167
-Is it a Mary Poppins thing?
-I might be making this up.

00:01:43.167 --> 00:01:46.868
But I think back in the day,
the man who invented --

00:01:46.868 --> 00:01:50.067
or the woman
who invented the umbrella,

00:01:50.067 --> 00:01:51.901
it was wooden,
wooden umbrellas.

00:01:51.901 --> 00:01:55.534
And so if you brought
them indoors, you would --

00:01:55.534 --> 00:01:58.067
If you opened them indoors,
you could dry them out.

00:01:58.067 --> 00:02:00.300
But if you kept them closed,
the wood would rot,

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.467
and you'd have to buy
another umbrella.

00:02:02.467 --> 00:02:05.601
So they told everyone
never open an umbrella indoors.

00:02:05.601 --> 00:02:07.501
So you leave them closed
and the wood would rot

00:02:07.501 --> 00:02:09.367
and you'd buy more umbrellas.
-So it was a way to keep --

00:02:09.367 --> 00:02:10.734
[ Laughter ]

00:02:10.734 --> 00:02:12.834
-Yeah!
-He believes me.

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:15.667
She believes me.
-It's kind of like...

00:02:15.667 --> 00:02:17.534
It's kind of like
what iPhone is now.

00:02:17.534 --> 00:02:21.934
[ Laughter ]

00:02:21.934 --> 00:02:24.968
-Don't update. Don't update or
else you got to buy another one.

00:02:24.968 --> 00:02:26.968
Yeah, exactly.
Tomorrow's Halloween.

00:02:26.968 --> 00:02:28.701
Are you excited for Halloween?

00:02:28.701 --> 00:02:30.100
-I could care less.
-Yeah.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:32.234
[ Laughter ]

00:02:32.234 --> 00:02:33.634
It's a big holiday.
And you have --

00:02:33.634 --> 00:02:37.968
-I mean, I'm an actor,
so I dress up like imaginary

00:02:37.968 --> 00:02:40.367
or other people all the time.
-That's true.

00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:41.901
This is not a holiday
for you, yeah.

00:02:41.901 --> 00:02:43.167
It's not a treat
for you to dress up.

00:02:43.167 --> 00:02:44.400
-It's like, "Give me my money."

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.334
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.334 --> 00:02:49.701
-Wow. You've really become --
You've really become corporate

00:02:49.701 --> 00:02:52.734
since I've had you on the show.
I've never seen you like this.

00:02:52.734 --> 00:02:56.367
You have two young daughters.

00:02:56.367 --> 00:03:00.234
As do I.
Are they into Halloween?

00:03:00.234 --> 00:03:02.167
-Oh, yeah. They love it.
They love it.

00:03:02.167 --> 00:03:04.234
I mean, my older daughter is 15,

00:03:04.234 --> 00:03:07.100
so she's kind of aging out
of the trick or treating thing.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:08.968
But we have an amazing house
in our neighborhood,

00:03:08.968 --> 00:03:13.367
the Halloween house that people
come from miles around

00:03:13.367 --> 00:03:16.334
to stare at this house.
And this guy who owns this house

00:03:16.334 --> 00:03:22.801
I think has like 125
animated figurines on his porch.

00:03:22.801 --> 00:03:24.400
And they're all
in different categories.

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:25.868
Like there's the zombie zone,

00:03:25.868 --> 00:03:29.300
the clown zone,
spooky little kid zone.

00:03:29.300 --> 00:03:31.868
His entire house is covered
with skeletons

00:03:31.868 --> 00:03:33.601
climbing up the side of it,

00:03:33.601 --> 00:03:36.000
and every year
he's got more stuff.

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:39.033
I just had one little guy, Sam,

00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:41.767
who's got like a pumpkin
for a head, and he --

00:03:41.767 --> 00:03:44.834
and if you step
on the little door pad,

00:03:44.834 --> 00:03:48.267
he starts laughing
and waving a lollipop at you.

00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:51.734
And I've seen
this terrify toddlers.

00:03:51.734 --> 00:03:52.701
Make them start crying.

00:03:52.701 --> 00:03:55.033
-Sam the lollipop
jack-o'-lantern?

00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:58.801
-Yeah, it's from some movie
that I never saw,

00:03:58.801 --> 00:04:00.434
but it's pretty scary.

00:04:00.434 --> 00:04:03.667
But anyway, I prefer --
that's the more fun part.

00:04:03.667 --> 00:04:05.934
-What are they dressing up as?

00:04:05.934 --> 00:04:08.734
-Marion --
I think -- It keeps changing.

00:04:08.734 --> 00:04:10.701
At last count it was --

00:04:10.701 --> 00:04:12.834
She was going to try
and dress up as midnight,

00:04:12.834 --> 00:04:17.167
which I thought was
rather lyrical, poetic.

00:04:17.167 --> 00:04:18.167
-That is actually
a beautiful thing.

00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:19.567
-Yeah.
She was going to have --

00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:21.434
-Like a Taylor Swift thing,
maybe like with "Midnights"?

00:04:21.434 --> 00:04:24.033
-I think. Yeah. I think that's
basically --

00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:25.367
[ Crowd cheers ]

00:04:25.367 --> 00:04:27.367
-My daughter --
-Taylor Swift.

00:04:27.367 --> 00:04:29.634
-My daughter
is going as Taylor Swift too.

00:04:29.634 --> 00:04:31.033
-Oh, really? Wow.
-Yeah.

00:04:31.033 --> 00:04:33.634
Different album.
-Which one?

00:04:33.634 --> 00:04:34.934
-1989.
-Oh.

00:04:34.934 --> 00:04:37.734
[ Crowd cheers ]

00:04:37.734 --> 00:04:39.734
That side of the room likes
"1989."

00:04:39.734 --> 00:04:41.000
That side of the room...
-Oh, wow.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:42.667
-Yeah, it's nothing
but competitions

00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:45.601
on "The Tonight Show" tonight.
-It's like a Rorschach test.

00:04:45.601 --> 00:04:46.968
-It really is like
a Rorschach test.

00:04:46.968 --> 00:04:49.801
Are you a Swiftie?
-Yeah.

00:04:49.801 --> 00:04:51.667
-Yeah.
[ Crowd cheers ]

00:04:51.667 --> 00:04:53.534
You have to be.
You kind of have to.

00:04:53.534 --> 00:04:54.868
Well, I know that
you're a musician.

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:56.234
-I am.
-You're a musician.

00:04:56.234 --> 00:04:58.367
And actually, you're doing
a really cool thing.

00:04:58.367 --> 00:05:00.601
Talk to me about this.
-Oh, dear.

00:05:00.601 --> 00:05:01.801
-You have a band?

00:05:01.801 --> 00:05:04.067
-Well, this fellow right here,
Jason Narducy,

00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:08.834
is an amazing singer-songwriter,
jack of all trades.

00:05:08.834 --> 00:05:11.267
And every once in a while
he says,

00:05:11.267 --> 00:05:13.367
"Hey, Mike, let's do a record."

00:05:13.367 --> 00:05:15.701
And we pick a record and we
play it from start to finish.

00:05:15.701 --> 00:05:18.667
So this year was
the 40th anniversary

00:05:18.667 --> 00:05:20.801
of R.E.M.'s record "Murmur."
-Whoo!

00:05:20.801 --> 00:05:23.667
-And yeah, and we
played it in --

00:05:23.667 --> 00:05:24.934
-Love "Murmur."

00:05:24.934 --> 00:05:28.934
-Yeah, we played it in Chicago
in the summertime,

00:05:28.934 --> 00:05:30.667
and it went pretty well.

00:05:30.667 --> 00:05:33.334
And then Jason was like, "Why
don't we take this on tour?"

00:05:33.334 --> 00:05:36.434
So he put it out there
to some clubs

00:05:36.434 --> 00:05:38.167
and a lot of people
got back to us,

00:05:38.167 --> 00:05:41.834
including in Athens,
Georgia, the 40 Watt Club,

00:05:41.834 --> 00:05:44.801
which is actually where
R.E.M. started.

00:05:44.801 --> 00:05:46.167
-That's right. Wow.

00:05:46.167 --> 00:05:48.167
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:05:48.167 --> 00:05:50.067
-And if you want to go
to that show, too bad

00:05:50.067 --> 00:05:52.067
because it's already sold out.
But some of the other shows...

00:05:52.067 --> 00:05:53.434
-You're giving out tickets or
something.

00:05:53.434 --> 00:05:55.868
-Yeah, are still available.
-Some of the other tickets

00:05:55.868 --> 00:05:57.067
are available?
-Yeah, yeah.

00:05:57.067 --> 00:06:00.267
-But have you ever
met anyone from R.E.M.?

00:06:00.267 --> 00:06:04.300
-I've been very blessed
to have met Michael Stipe.

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:06.000
Very, very sweet man.
[ Crowd cheers ]

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:07.501
And yeah.
-He's unbelievable.

00:06:07.501 --> 00:06:08.868
-He's an unbelievably talented
person.

00:06:08.868 --> 00:06:10.367
-He's actually an unbelievable
human being.

00:06:10.367 --> 00:06:16.100
-Yeah. And Mike Mills actually
showed up at the Chicago show.

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.434
-What? Did you freak out?

00:06:18.434 --> 00:06:20.234
-I freaked out. He walked in.
Basically,

00:06:20.234 --> 00:06:22.767
we were in the green room
going through our set list.

00:06:22.767 --> 00:06:24.267
-You're playing R.E.M.?
-Yeah.

00:06:24.267 --> 00:06:27.534
-Ins and outs
of the R.E.M. show, yeah.

00:06:27.534 --> 00:06:31.334
And right before we're about
to bound onto the stage,

00:06:31.334 --> 00:06:32.834
Mike Mills walks
in the green room.

00:06:32.834 --> 00:06:34.100
-You're kidding me.

00:06:34.100 --> 00:06:38.400
-And everybody just kind of
gets, like, jittery,

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:41.868
And he's like, "Hey, I'm just --
I'm so excited to be here.

00:06:41.868 --> 00:06:44.100
And I know you guys
must have worked so hard."

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:46.434
And he had a blast.
And he was really nice.

00:06:46.434 --> 00:06:48.567
He hung out afterwards
and was very --

00:06:48.567 --> 00:06:51.000
-Did he get up and play
or anything?

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.133
-He got up and sang
a little bit.

00:06:53.133 --> 00:06:54.601
Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-He did?

00:06:54.601 --> 00:06:56.200
-Yeah, yeah.
-See, that's what I'm
talking about.

00:06:56.200 --> 00:06:57.801
That's a good --
-Now, I don't want to guarantee

00:06:57.801 --> 00:07:00.300
that that's
going to happen ever again.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.567
-But at least you can guarantee
at Athens, Georgia.

00:07:02.567 --> 00:07:03.567
That's definitely
going to happen.

00:07:03.567 --> 00:07:04.701
-Mike Mills will be there.

00:07:04.701 --> 00:07:06.400
-We'll see. We'll see.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:08.534
-"Murmur."
What have you got there?

00:07:08.534 --> 00:07:10.400
What song you got off of murmur
that I would know?

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:12.033
It starts with
"Radio Free Europe."

00:07:12.033 --> 00:07:13.534
-Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:13.534 --> 00:07:15.934
♪ Calling out in transit ♪

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:18.767
♪ Calling out in transit ♪

00:07:18.767 --> 00:07:21.234
-That's not gonna happen.

00:07:21.234 --> 00:07:23.167
That's not gonna happen.

00:07:23.167 --> 00:07:24.868
Which date do you want?

00:07:24.868 --> 00:07:26.667
You can have one of these dates.
-I can go one of these dates?

00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:28.834
-Yeah, yeah.
-I don't want to perform.

00:07:28.834 --> 00:07:32.000
-You don't?
D.C., The Black Cat maybe.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:33.901
-I'll be at The Black Cat.
-Yeah, yeah. Cool.

00:07:33.901 --> 00:07:35.067
-Meorw.
-Meorw.

00:07:35.067 --> 00:07:36.234
[ Laughter ]

00:07:36.234 --> 00:07:37.467
-I know that club.

00:07:37.467 --> 00:07:39.234
Dude, this is fantastic
that you do this, though.

00:07:39.234 --> 00:07:41.234
-Yeah.
-I love that --

00:07:41.234 --> 00:07:43.200
I have to get to
these other projects

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:46.133
that you're doing also.
"Waiting for Godot."

00:07:46.133 --> 00:07:48.167
I would be excited
to see you in this play.

00:07:48.167 --> 00:07:49.267
-Yeah.
-Samuel Beckett.

00:07:49.267 --> 00:07:50.434
This is a classic.

00:07:50.434 --> 00:07:53.601
-It is.
-Have you ever done this before?

00:07:53.601 --> 00:07:55.133
-No, I've never done
"Waiting for Godot."

00:07:55.133 --> 00:07:57.300
But I saw it
when I was a little boy,

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:01.667
and I remember being mesmerized
by it,

00:08:01.667 --> 00:08:04.300
and I kind of credit it with

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:06.901
one of the reasons I got into
theater was seeing that show

00:08:06.901 --> 00:08:08.701
when I was a kid.
-Wow.

00:08:08.701 --> 00:08:10.234
And now you're in it.
-And now I'm doing it.

00:08:10.234 --> 00:08:14.234
-Yeah. That's wild.
And you play the young man

00:08:14.234 --> 00:08:15.734
with the pumpkin head
and the lollipop?

00:08:15.734 --> 00:08:19.367
-Yes, yes. I come to scare --
[ Laughter ]

00:08:19.367 --> 00:08:21.501
-Sam?
-No.

00:08:21.501 --> 00:08:23.000
-I'm playing Estragon,

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.400
which is unfortunately
very close to estrogen.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:28.000
But it's not estrogen,
it's Estragon.

00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.133
-Estragon. Estragon.
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:30.133 --> 00:08:33.000
And then my buddy Paul Sparks,
he's playing Vladimir

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:35.834
and he's -- we're old friends
and we've done a lot of work

00:08:35.834 --> 00:08:37.734
together over the years.
-It's a brilliant play.

00:08:37.734 --> 00:08:41.901
It's very funny and very --
makes you think.

00:08:41.901 --> 00:08:44.434
Hey, by the way,
your directorial debut.

00:08:44.434 --> 00:08:46.334
Congratulations.
You're now a film director.

00:08:46.334 --> 00:08:47.501
-I did, yeah.
I directed a movie.

00:08:47.501 --> 00:08:48.667
[ Cheers and applause ]
Thank you.

00:08:48.667 --> 00:08:51.434
-You did a great job.
-Oh, thank you.

00:08:51.434 --> 00:08:53.234
-"Eric LaRue."
-"Eric LaRue."

00:08:53.234 --> 00:08:57.767
-"Eric LaRue" is the name of
the film, and it's a --

00:08:57.767 --> 00:09:00.267
How do we explain
what the film is about?

00:09:00.267 --> 00:09:03.200
-Well, very briefly,
it's about the parents

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.634
of a young man
who commits a school shooting.

00:09:05.634 --> 00:09:09.801
And at the start of the film,
he's in prison.

00:09:09.801 --> 00:09:13.400
And it's really focusing
on the parents trying to deal

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:15.434
with the aftermath
of what he's done

00:09:15.434 --> 00:09:20.033
and how -- the repercussions
it has on their lives

00:09:20.033 --> 00:09:21.601
and their community.

00:09:21.601 --> 00:09:23.901
-And Judy Greer?

00:09:23.901 --> 00:09:25.901
-Judy Greer. Oh, my God.
-Knockout.

00:09:25.901 --> 00:09:27.334
Knockout performance.

00:09:27.334 --> 00:09:28.934
-You never seen --
-Knockout performance.

00:09:28.934 --> 00:09:30.701
Everybody is fantastic
in this.

00:09:30.701 --> 00:09:32.734
-Alexander Skarsgard.
-Skarsgard.

00:09:32.734 --> 00:09:36.033
Yeah, it's great.
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:09:36.033 --> 00:09:38.868
Here's a scene from Michael
Shannon's directorial debut,

00:09:38.868 --> 00:09:41.000
"Eric LaRue." Take a look.

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:43.534
-But the Lord.

00:09:43.534 --> 00:09:46.934
Oh, he's the only one
who can judge.

00:09:46.934 --> 00:09:48.934
And the only thing you should do
is leave yourself open

00:09:48.934 --> 00:09:50.167
to healing and pray.

00:09:50.167 --> 00:09:52.434
-Oh, stop it!
-Stop what?

00:09:52.434 --> 00:09:54.100
-Stop talking to me like that!

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:57.000
I'm not in Sunday school!
I'm not a child!

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.133
-I don't think you understand.

00:09:58.133 --> 00:10:00.868
-I understand more
than you think I do.

00:10:00.868 --> 00:10:02.334
-I don't think you know
what you think.

00:10:02.334 --> 00:10:05.100
-Do you think I'm stupid?

00:10:05.100 --> 00:10:07.267
-Ooh.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:07.267 --> 00:10:09.701
Michael Shannon, everybody.

00:10:09.701 --> 00:10:11.567
Congratulations.
Always great to see you.

00:10:11.567 --> 00:10:13.300
-Thank you.
-"Waiting for Godot" premieres

00:10:13.300 --> 00:10:16.467
this Saturday at
the Polonsky Shakespeare Center.

00:10:16.467 --> 00:10:18.033
More "Tonight Show"
after the break.

00:10:18.033 --> 00:10:19.133
Stick around, everybody.

